ive-canonical-names name -> canonical name for intentional aliases\\n1095\\t     :modules       module registry value (see `yin.vm.module`)\\n1096\\t     :make-stream   (fn [capacity] -> create outcome); no default\\n1097\\t     :call-in       explicit inbound request handle\\n1098\\t     :call-out      explicit outbound response handle\\n1099\\t     :call-out-cursor, :ffi-caller-id  `yin.vm/empty-state`'s\\n1100\\t     :call-capacity capacity for a constructed FFI pair\\n1101\\t     :bridge        host FFI handlers\\n1102\\t\\n1103\\t   There is no `:in-stream`: program observation belongs to\\n1104\\t   `dao.stream.observer`, and an obsolete `:in-stream` option is\\n1105\\t   rejected here, before any FFI resource is allocated.\\n1106\\t\\n1107\\t   Construction is all-or-nothing: creating the FFI pair and minting the\\n1108\\t   call-out cursor are stream operations, and any non-`ok` outcome fails here\\n1109\\t   rather than leaving a half-built VM. The bridge cursor is minted by\\n1110\\t   `ffi/attach` below; the program cursor belongs to observer attachment.\\\"\\n1111\\t  ([] (create-vm {}))\\n1112\\t  ([opts]\\n1113\\t   (when (contains? opts :in-stream)\\n1114\\t     (throw (ex-info\\n1115\\t              \\\"Program observation moved to dao.stream.observer: a VM no longer accepts :in-stream\\\"\\n1116\\t              {:in-stream (:in-stream opts)})))\\n1117\\t   (let [env (vm/check-bindings! :env (or (:env opts) {}))\\n1118\\t         base (vm/empty-state\\n1119\\t                (assoc (select-keys opts\\n1120\\t                                    [:primitives :primitive-profiles\\n1121\\t                                     :primitive-canonical-names :modules\\n1122\\t                                     :make-stream :call-in :call-out\\n1123\\t                                     :call-out-cursor :ffi-caller-id\\n1124\\t                                     :call-capacity :link-request\\n1125\\t                                     :link-response :origin :ancestry\\n1126\\t                                     :capability-secret :secret-source\\n1127\\t                                     :attach-stream :gc-threshold])\\n1128\\t                       :telemetry (:telemetry opts)\\n1129\\t                       :vm-model :ast-walker))]\\n1130\\t     (-> (map->ASTWalkerVM (merge base\\n1131\\t                                  {:bridge nil,\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345\",\"uuid\":\"9f16f8ab-36b0-4d80-94f7-18d5d0d7631c\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-18T16:33:06.065Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc\",\"content\":\"(defn create-vm\\n  \\\"Create a new ASTWalkerVM.\\n\\n   Options:\\n     :env           initial lexical environment\\n     :primitives    primitive operations map\\n     :primitive-profiles published primitive profile registry\\n     :primitive-canonical-names name -> canonical name for intentional aliases\\n     :modules       module registry value (see `yin.vm.module`)\\n     :make-stream   (fn [capacity] -> create outcome); no default\\n     :call-in       explicit inbound request handle\\n     :call-out      explicit outbound response handle\\n     :call-out-cursor, :ffi-caller-id  `yin.vm/empty-state`'s\\n     :call-capacity capacity for a constructed FFI pair\\n     :bridge        host FFI handlers\\n\\n   There is no `:in-stream`: program observation belongs to\\n   `dao.stream.observer`, and an obsolete `:in-stream` option is\\n   rejected here, before any FFI resource is allocated.\\n\\n   Construction is all-or-nothing: creating the FFI pair and minting the\\n   call-out cursor are stream operations, and any non-`ok` outcome fails here\\n   rather than leaving a half-built VM. The bridge cursor is minted by\\n   `ffi/attach` below; the program cursor belongs to observer attachment.\\\"\\n  ([] (create-vm {}))\\n  ([opts]\\n   (when (contains? opts :in-stream)\\n     (throw (ex-info\\n              \\\"Program observation moved to dao.stream.observer: a VM no longer accepts :in-stream\\\"\\n              {:in-stream (:in-stream opts)})))\\n   (let [env (vm/check-bindings! :env (or (:env opts) {}))\\n         base (vm/empty-state\\n                (assoc (select-keys opts\\n                                    [:primitives :primitive-profiles\\n                                     :primitive-canonical-names :modules\\n                                     :make-stream :call-in :call-out\\n                                     :call-out-cursor :ffi-caller-id\\n                                     :call-capacity :link-request\\n                                     :link-response :origin :ancestry\\n                                     :capability-secret :secret-source\\n                                     :attach-stream :gc-threshold])\\n                       :telemetry (:telemetry opts)\\n                       :vm-model :ast-walker))]\\n     (-> (map->ASTWalkerVM (merge base\\n                                  {:bridge nil,\",\"numLines\":45,\"startLine\":1087,\"totalLines\":1275}},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"call_1c673b3515724ce2bd7341d2\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795018441000-engineer-fix-constructed-collection-order-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:40919:{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"msg_20261119003306e2d6d8d0282248a6\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Good. Now let me design and write the actual test namespace. This is the big artifact. Let me carefully plan each test family with concrete code.\\n\\n### Namespace `yang.clojure-collection-order-test`\\n\\n**Helpers (independent builders):**\\n\\n```clojure\\n(def ^:private origin [:source :program \\\"batch-1\\\" 0])\\n\\n(def ^:private registry\\n  (-> (module/empty-registry)\\n      module/register-cell-module\\n      data/register-data-module))\\n\\n(def ^:private opts\\n  {:make-stream tu/make-stream\\n   :capability-secret tu/secret\\n   :primitives vm/primitives\\n   :modules registry})\\n\\n(defn- load-semantic-ast (vm/fresh-code-loader semantic/load-ast vm/ast-contract))\\n\\n(def ^:private runners\\n  {:ast-walker (fn [ast] (vm/eval (tu/create-vm opts) ast))\\n   :semantic (fn [ast] (vm/run (load-semantic-ast (semantic/create-vm opts) (vm/ast->datoms ast))))\\n   :stack (fn [ast] (vm/run (dvm/create-vm (:image (derive/stack-image (:vector (linearize/project ast)))) (assoc opts :contract vm/stack-contract))))\\n   :register (fn [ast] (vm/run (rvm/create-vm (derive/register-image (:vector (linearize/project ast))) (assoc opts :contract vm/register-contract))))})\\n\\n(defn- run-everywhere [form]\\n  (let [ast (yang/compile form)]\\n    (into {} (map (fn [[k run]] [k (vm/value (run ast))])) runners)))\\n\\n(defn- run-rows [rows]  ;; legacy execution\\n  (let [vm (ast-walker/create-vm opts)]\\n    (ast-walker/vm-load-rows vm rows vm/ast-contract) ... hmm vm-load-rows returns updated vm\\n    (vm/run updated)))\\n```\\n\\nWait — `vm/eval` protocol: `(vm/eval vm ast)`. And `vm/run` — protocol run. OK mirror call_test exactly.\\n\\n**Constructor finding:**\\n\\n```clojure\\n(defn- ctor?\\n  [n seed op]\\n  (and (map? n) (= :application (:type n))\\n       (= :variable (get-in n [:operator :type]))\\n       (= op (get-in n [:operator :name]))\\n       (= :literal (get-in n [:operands 0 :type]))\\n       (= seed (get-in n [:operands 0 :value]))))\\n\\n(defn- constructors\\n  \\\"Every constructed-collection application of `ast`, outermost first.\\\"\\n  [ast]\\n  (let [acc (atom [])]\\n    (walk/postwalk (fn [n] (when (and (ctor? n {} 'assoc) (or (ctor? n [] 'conj) (ctor? n #{} 'conj))) ...))))\\n```\\n\\nHmm simpler: collect nodes where operator is assoc+seed {} OR conj+seed [] OR conj+seed #{}:\\n\\n```clojure\\n(defn- collection-applications\\n  [ast]\\n  (let [acc (atom [])]\\n    (walk/postwalk\\n      (fn [n]\\n        (when (and (map? n)\\n                   (= :application (:type n))\\n                   (= :variable (get-in n [:operator :type]))\\n                   (contains? #{'assoc 'conj} (get-in n [:operator :name]))\\n                   (= :literal (get-in n [:operands 0 :type]))\\n                   (contains? #{{} [] #{}} (get-in n [:operands 0 :value]))\\n                   (case [(get-in n [:operator :name]) (get-in n [:operands 0 :value])]\\n                     ['assoc {}] true\\n                     ['conj []] true\\n                     ['conj #{}] true\\n                     false))\\n          (swap! acc conj n))\\n        n)\\n      ast)\\n    @acc))\\n```\\n\\nCareful: `(= {} (get-in ...))` for seed — `{}` is a map; `(contains? #{{} [] #{}} v)` uses hash — fine.\\n\\nWait — one flaw: `(conj [] ...)` for a dynamic VECTOR has seed `[]`; a user's EXPLICIT `(conj [] x)` call would ALSO match (operator variable conj, first operand literal [])! For order controls I need to distinguish the emitter's output from explicit calls. For explicit `assoc` control: `(assoc {} k v)` — operator 'assoc, operands [lit {} ...] — MATCHES ctor?. So my collection finder would pick up explicit calls. For the CONTROL tests (written order), that's actually what I want to find (the explicit call node) — but I must distinguish. Distinguish by source? The explicit call node is compiled by compile-application — indistinguishable structurally! I'll handle by context: in control tests, compile `(let [t tap] (assoc {} (t :k1) ...))` — the ctor-finder returns possibly several nodes; the explicit assoc call is the ONLY constructor in that program (no literals maps elsewhere) → unambiguous. In emitter tests, programs contain no explicit assoc/conj calls. I'll note that in docstrings.\\n\\n**Sortedness oracle:**\\n\\n```clojure\\n(defn- child-roots\\n  \\\"The canonical root of each operand, projected independently of the emitter.\\\"\\n  [node]\\n  (mapv (fn [o] (:root (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode o))) (:operands node)))\\n\\n(defn- pair-keys\\n  \\\"The `[key-root value-root]` sort keys of a map constructor's pairs.\\\"\\n  [node]\\n  (mapv vec (partition 2 (rest (child-roots node)))))\\n\\n(defn- sorted-ascending? [ks]\\n  (every? (fn [[a b]] (neg? (jing/canonical-compare a b))) (partition 2 1 ks)))\\n\\n(defn- element-keys [node] (mapv vector (rest (child-roots node))))\\n```\\n\\n**Snapshot (equality digest, cohort model):**\\n\\n```clojure\\n(defn- project-batch [ast token]\\n  (encoder/project (encoder/source-envelope :cc4/replay token [ast])))\\n\\n(defn- snapshot\\n  [{:keys [tree rows]}]\\n  (let [c1 (vec (sort jing/canonical-compare (filter #(= :yang/call-site (nth % 3)) rows)))\\n        keys (vec (sort jing/canonical-compare\\n                        (into [] (comp (filter #(= :yang/call-site (nth % 3)))\\n                                       (map #(vec (take 3 %)))) rows)))\\n        sem (linearize/project-rows tree nil rows)\\n        stack (derive/stack-image (:vector sem) {...})\\n        register (derive/register-image (:vector sem) {...})]\\n    {:root (:root tree)\\n     :keys-digest (jing/content-hash keys)\\n     :c1-digest (jing/content-hash c1)\\n     :provenance-digest (jing/content-hash [(:call-provenance sem) (:call-provenance stack) (:call-provenance register)])}))\\n```\\n\\nWait — cohort's `replay-snapshot` uses `marked` fn (their local). I'll write my own `keys` extraction: `[o r path]` triples (the cohort's `marked` returns exactly `[o r path]` — actually `(map (fn [[o r path _ _]] [o r path]))`). For permutation invariance, including origin [o] is fine (same origin for all permutations).\\n\\nHmm wait — do I even need C1 metadata for permutation comparison? The permutation corpus compiles the same logical entries — the C1 markers etc. all same. Including them strengthens the check. OK.\\n\\nFor stack/register images: need `:segment-address` + `:call-provenance` opts like the cohort:\\n\\n```clojure\\n(derive/stack-image (:vector sem) {:segment-address :fixorder/stack :call-provenance (:call-provenance sem)})\\n```\\n\\n**Permutation machinery:**\\n\\n```clojure\\n(defn- permutations\\n  \\\"All orderings of (range n), the plain recursive enumeration (no host rand).\\\"\\n  [n]\\n  (if (zero? n) [[]]\\n      (mapcat (fn [i] (map #(into [i] %) (permutations (dec n)))) ... )))\\n```\\nStandard insertion or selection recursion. For n=0..4 → 1,1,2,6,24 vectors.\\n\\n```clojure\\n(defn- rotated\\n  \\\"The `k`-th rotation of `v`.\\\"\\n  [k v] (into (subvec v (mod k (count v))) (subvec v 0 (mod k (count v)))))\\n```\\n\\n```clojure\\n(defn- lcg-shuffle\\n  \\\"A deterministic recorded-seed shuffle: the explicitly specified generator\\n   (ruling RULING 3 — no host rand, no host hash). x_{n+1} = (1103515245 x_n +\\n   12345) mod 2^31, seed recorded per corpus; a Fisher-Yates pass over the\\n   indices uses successive states.\\\"\\n  [seed v]\\n  ...)\\n```\\nImplement with unchecked math on fixnums? On cljs/cljd numbers... (mod (+ (* 1103515245 x) 12345) 2147483648) — 1103515245*x can exceed 2^53 for x near 2^31 (product ~2.4e18 > 2^53) — precision loss on doubles → but DETERMINISTIC across hosts (all hosts use doubles; JVM longs would differ from doubles!). JVM: (* 1103515245 2147483647) = ~2.37e18 — fits in long exactly, but as double loses precision → JVM long math ≠ cljs double math → DIFFERENT SHUFFLES ON DIFFERENT HOSTS → test divergence! Must keep the generator's arithmetic within exact-double range: x < 2^21 before multiply? Use a multiplier that keeps products < 2^53: e.g. x_{n+1} = (48271 * x) mod (2^31 - 1) (Lehmer RNG): 48271 * (2^31-2) ≈ 1.036e14 < 2^53 ✓ exact in doubles on every host. mod 2147483646. Seed recorded. \\n\\nFisher-Yates downward with `(mod state n)` index... All arithmetic ≤ 2^53 → portable.\\n\\n**Corpus (family 2):** recorded entries; each entry = vector of `[k v]` pairs with forms; map built per permutation via `(apply array-map (interleave ks vs))` / `(apply hash-map ...)`; plus meta variants. Each corpus case: `{:id ... :entries [[k v]...] :dynamic? true}`.\\n\\nEntries must be COMPILED forms — i.e., the entries hold FORMS (symbols, lists). e.g.:\\n\\n```clojure\\n(def ^:private map-corpus\\n  [[:id :constant-keys-dynamic-values\\n    :entries [[(quote :a) (quote (inc-ish...))]]]\\n   ...])\\n```\\n\\nWait — dynamic values must compile: use `(+ v 1)` forms referencing a bound local? Standalone `yang/compile` of `{:a (+ x 1)}` — variable x free — compiles fine (variable node); execution would need env but permutation tests only compare COMPILATION outputs (no execution). Free variables are fine for compilation! So entries can use plain forms with free symbols. \\n\\nCorpus cases (each with ≥1 dynamic element):\\n1. `:constant-keys` — keys :a :b, values `(x :a)`-style dynamic — e.g. `[[(quote :k0) '(+ n 0)] [(quote :k1) '(+ n 1)]]`.\\n2. `:dynamic-keys` — keys `'(+ n 0)`, `'(+ n 1)`; values constants.\\n3. `:heterogeneous-keys` — keys: `:kw`, `\\\"str\\\"`, `3`, `1.5`, `'sym`, `'(quote [1 2])`, `'true`, `'nil`? nil key — allowed in Clojure maps. Dynamic requirement: one value dynamic. Include quoted collection keys: `'(quote {:a 1})`? A quoted MAP as a key — the quote is stripped inside the emitter (nested quote) — becomes literal data. ✓ Also `^:meta` quoted key for semantic-metadata preservation: `'^{:sem true} [1 2]`? Hmm — with-meta on quoted form: `(quote ^{:sem true} [1 2])` — inside quote, meta is part of data? When the READER reads `^{:sem true} [1 2]` it attaches meta to the vector; quote preserves it as data metadata. Constructed programmatically: `(with-meta [1 2] {:sem true})` inside quote: `'(quote ~(with-meta ...))` — no syntax-quote; build the form as data: `(list 'quote (with-meta [1 2] {:sem true}))`. Compile: nested in map → compile-form → compile-quote → literal with value carrying meta → data payload keeps non-position metadata → affects content. ✓\\n4. `:nested-collections` — values are nested dynamic maps/sets/vectors: `{(:in ) ...}` e.g. entries [[(quote :nest) '{:inner (+ n 1)}] ...] wait a nested map with dynamic content: the VALUE form is a map `{:inner (+ n 1)}` (dynamic → constructed). ✓\\n5. `:floats` — keys/values with 1.5, 2.25, floats as constants.\\n6. `:duplicate-subtrees` — two entries with the same VALUE form `(identity n)` → identical value roots (tie-break via different keys — value-root secondary). And also two IDENTICAL full pairs (tie case — see family 4).\\n7. `:destructuring-names` — covered by the fn-context case (family 6 / separate).\\n8. `:reader-meta` — forms carrying position meta (from being written in the test file they already carry meta! quoted forms read from the test file carry :line/:column — good, that's \\\"reader metadata\\\" naturally; plus explicit `(with-meta ...)` non-position `:note`).\\n\\nFor sizes 8/9/16/32: generated entries `:k0..:kN` → values `(quote (v n i))`? Entries constructed by enumeration — recorded generator (deterministic enumeration is data-equivalent). Keys `:k0`..`:k31`, values `(+ n i)` forms → each value root distinct. Permutations: forward, reverse, rotations 1..3, lcg-shuffle with recorded seeds.\\n\\nAssertion per case: for every representation & permutation → snapshot identical across all (and root equality), PLUS sortedness oracle on each compiled constructor. Pre-fix RED: snapshots differ between permutations (JVM). Post-fix GREEN.\\n\\nNote: 8/9 cross the array-map→hash-map threshold (8 = array-map max on JVM; 9+ = hash-map). Representations: `(apply array-map ...)` and `(apply hash-map ...)` for each size (including 8/9/16/32 — array-map with 32 entries on JVM stays array-map (PersistentArrayMap accepts any count? `(apply array-map (range 64))` → PersistentArrayMap — yes always)).\\n\\n cljs array-map: PROMOTES to hash-map beyond 8 entries at READ time for literals, but `(apply array-map ...)` on cljs — cljs `array-map` returns PMAP? In ClojureScript, `(apply array-map kv...)` always returns a persistent array map (no promotion in array-map constructor). OK.\\n\\nRepresentation forms: also the plain map LITERAL from the test file `{...}` — that's just one more array/hash map per host reader thresholds. I'll include a literal variant where sizes are small.\\n\\n**Family 3 (sets):** same machinery with `#{...}` forms — entries = elements. Representations: `(set [elems])`? Hmm — host set from vector: `(set v)` insertion→hash. Also literal `#{}` in file. Build via `(set (vec perm))`? But set content identical; iteration differs by insertion — construct with `(apply hash-set ...)` and sorted-vector set. Sizes/permutations same as maps. Elements: mixtures as above. Nested dynamic sets: element = another dynamic set form `#{(+ n 1)}`. Duplicate VALUE collisions: two DIFFERENT expressions evaluating to same value — `(mod n 2)` and `(mod n 4)`? eval not needed — just compiled: distinct element roots, both occurrences retained: assert constructor has 2 element operands. For \\\"same-value\\\" runtime assertions — family 5.\\n\\n**Family 4 (oracle):** \\n- sortedness+pairing+seed-first on corpus constructors: for maps assert `(= (first roots) seed-root)`? Seed-first: operand 0 literal {} and pair-keys sorted.\\n- pairing intact: multiset of pair keys equals multiset of [compile(k), compile(v)] roots compiled independently: for corpus entries compile each entry k,v standalone `(yang/compile k)` — roots — then compare SORTED multisets... careful: compiled standalone vs in-tree equal roots (established). Assert `(= (sort jing/canonical-compare expected-pair-keys) (sort jing/canonical-compare actual))` — pairing+no-loss. \\n- value-root secondary: two entries same key form, different values → tie on key-root broken by value-root: `{(+ n 1) :a (+ n 1) :b}` → pair keys [[kr :a-root] [kr :b-root]] sorted by value → assert order follows value order (and both occurrences present).\\n- full ties: `{x :v x :v}` where both pairs compile identically → 2 occurrences preserved (count operands), roots equal → \\\"no occurrence loss\\\".\\n- metadata invariance: `(with-meta form {:line 1 :column 2})` vs plain vs `{:note :x}` — same root, same operand order.\\n- literal payload metadata preservation: quoted-collection key with `:sem true` meta → different root than same key without meta (metadata IS content in data payloads), order still sorted.\\n\\n**Family 5 (execution):** via run-everywhere:\\n- key-before-value & canonical order trace: env `{'tap fn}` — run `{(tap :k1) (tap :v1) (tap :k2) (tap :v2)}` on all runners, log = flattened canonical-sorted pairs (derived via independent builder from the COMPILED tree: compute pair order, expected trace = concat of [k-arg v-arg]...). Wait — the tap ARGUMENT values determine log entries; the expected order derived from canonical sort of pair keys. The tap function echoes its arg. Expected trace: interleave per sorted pair. Compare actual log.\\n- collision: canonically-last wins: `{(- n 1) :b (+ n 1) :a}` with env n=1 → wait env n → `(run-everywhere-opts form env)`. Need env support in runners — extend run-everywhere with env merged into opts. Then expected winner = value of last pair per independent sort; assert both orders (also compile the swapped insertion — same result post-fix).\\n- set collisions: `(let ...)`? sets: `#{(+ n 1) (- n 1) 2 3}` with n=1 → elements 2,2,2,3 → runtime set #{2 3}; compiled retains 4 element operands.\\n- no duplication: log has each event once per occurrence.\\n- failure stops later evaluations: `{(tap :k1) (tap :v1) (throwing :k2) :v2}` — hmm throwing op: use `(/ 1 0)`? checked-divide raises. Trace: events before failure only. On walkers, an exception propagates as ex-info? `av-thrown` pattern catches ExceptionInfo. For the runners, an error mid-program — walker raises ex-info; assert log prefix + the run throws. Simpler: use tap for trace and a `boom` env fn that thr