not native → ordinary application ✓.)\\n  For lower-unit: the form must be a top-level program: `[(list 'def 'm M)]`? lower-unit publishes globals — `def m {…}` — the map compiles inside. ✓\\n- macro expansion: defmacro whose expansion BUILDS a constructed map — e.g. defmacro `pairs->map` that returns `(assoc {} ~@...)`? RULING: macro-generated form maps re-enter compile-form through this same emitter. Test: a macro returning a map FORM constructed at expansion: \\n  ```\\n  (defmacro build-map [k v] {(quote k) v})  ; hmm macro body builds host map\\n  ```\\n  A defmacro body that constructs a host map `{k v}` → macro's fn at expansion time builds the map form → expansion output is a map literal with dynamic content → compiled via emitter → canonical order. Then expand-like-the-repl: status :ok, expansion origins not fabricated (every origin is admission or recorded event), tree contains canonically-ordered constructor. The cohort keeps the production twin-call fixture — my namespace adds this focused one.\\n\\n**Family 7 (legacy):**\\n- Load CBOR fixture (pre-fix artifact) → rows; assert recorded old root; `vm-load-rows` + run on walker lane (and semantic lane?) → value = recorded old result {2 :a}; recompile same source now → different root (order changed) and new result {2 :b}; old artifact untouched (its rows still validate: `vm/semantic-bytecode->ast` runs inside load).\\n- Keep unannotated legacy-load/missing-C1 refusals — they live in the callsite battery (untouched; I'll note in findings).\\n- two different tokens → different origins/keys: compile+project same content with tokens \\\"token-a\\\"/\\\"token-b\\\" → roots equal, origin sets differ (mirror cohort negative member assertion).\\n\\n**Family 1 (arity maps):** as analyzed:\\n- argument-vector + lower-unit members with cohort source; find named arity map constructor (operand literals contain :yin.k/fn); assert:\\n  - literal key order [:yin.k/fn :yin.vm.data/reason :yin.k/argc] — the ruled order;\\n  