 Symbols? rows contain no symbols... :variable rows contain symbols ('assoc). `:sym` slots in rows hold SYMBOLS (e.g. row [id :variable assoc]). CBOR encode of symbols — the Jing profile supports symbols? canonical-bytes hashes ASTs containing symbols... dao.jing.cbor supports symbols? The cc4-replay-jvm.cbor contains full trees incl. symbols — it was written with cbor encode ✓ (r2: \\\"Binary canonical CBOR retains even origins whose printed keyword cannot be re-read as EDN\\\"). OK symbols fine.\\n\\nEnv {'fn value}? No env needed.\\n\\nAlso record the RUNTIME VALUE {2 :a} — a plain map — CBOR ✓.\\n\\nExecution portability of the fixture loader: read BYTES per host: clj `(Files/readAllBytes (Path-of path))`? simpler: `(.readAllBytes (java.nio.file.Paths/get path (make-array String 0)))`. cljs fs.readFileSync → Buffer/Uint8Array ✓. cljd File.readAsBytesSync ✓. jolt → :clj branch (Paths) — jolt runs on its own host with java.nio available? Jolt is a Clojure-like on Chez? The jolt host... cbor_fixtures used slurp for :clj — plain slurp returns String (bad for bytes). For bytes on clj: use `(java.nio.file.Files/readAllBytes ...)`. Jolt: java interop?? Jolt is NOT JVM — `:clj` branch on jolt would break IF my namespace runs there. Hmm — jolt provides its own host IO? The jolt smoke used defcfn... Risky. Jolt lane can't run anyway until the manifest owner unblocks (recorded refusal), but my namespace should still LOAD on jolt eventually. I'll gate the file-reading part: make the legacy test read bytes via a small helper with #?(:clj java.nio, :cljs fs, :cljd dart-io) — jolt takes :clj. If jolt lacks java.nio, the legacy test would fail there — record as known boundary in findings (jolt lane currently refuses at manifest anyway). Hmm — better: don't hard-require java.nio; on :clj slurping bytes: `(with-open [in (clojure.java.io/input-stream path)] ...)` — also JVM-specific. It's fine: jolt acceptance is already blocked by the owner dependency; I'll note the IO dependency