sis. A fresh-JVM probe (recorded at `collab/yang-clojure/1795033280000-fixorder-r2-evidence/fixorder-r2-nan-set-probe.md`) settled the actual semantics: `into` over the NaN pair keeps both (with number/string/keyword/vector thirds too), `(set …)` keeps both, `hash-set` and persistent `conj` chains collapse, and the map analogues split the same way — **retention is per-constructor, not map-vs-set**. One wrinkle beyond the reviewer's disposition: a *single* transient `into` session mixing a **seq** element with the pair loses one NaN on the JVM (hash collision ruled out: 2146959360 vs −1050398832), so the block builds `(conj (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN]) (list '+ 'n 0))` — stable across fresh JVMs and different seqs; nothing collapses on Node/Dart (`===`/Dart `==`). My r1 failure was self-inflicted: count assertions calibrated to a collapsed set failed on actual *retention with reordering*. The block now asserts 3 element occurrences survive, the tied root appears exactly twice (no emitter dedup), the third distinct occurrence is retained, and the element vector equals its own non-descending canonical sort — the tie-certification form.\n\n## F2 / F3\n\n`ascending?` → `strictly-ascending?` (definition + 15 call sites), docstring stating it does not certify ties and pointing to the sort-equality form; the no-tie block reti\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
